R01=dict(id='R01',slug='how-i-got-into-research',title='I Want to Tell You How I Got Into Research',
 hook='Most people do undergraduate research for a portfolio. I did it for a different reason.',
 abstract=[('BACKGROUND','A lot of people get into undergraduate research for the sole purpose of building a portfolio. But I saw it from a different perspective.'),
 ('METHODS','A drop year, and an individual who believed in me quite a lot.'),
 ('RESULTS','I joined college and forgot all the whys and hows of why I got there.'),
 ('CONCLUSIONS','These ideas would eventually become the foundation of my interest in research.')],
 keywords='undergraduate research; medical education; preventive medicine; nutrition',
 highlight=None,
 body=[
 ('h','Why I did not see it as a portfolio'),
 'A lot of people get into undergraduate research for the sole purpose of building a portfolio so that they can go abroad, write the USMLE exam, or see it as a clearance aspect for their PLAB or AMC exams.',
 'But I saw it from a different perspective.',
 'It all started with how I got into MBBS in the first place.',
 ('h','The person who believed in me'),
 'I studied at PC Thomas Classes, and just before my drop year, I was influenced by an individual who believed in me quite a lot.',
 'He told me all about whole-food, plant-based diets and veganism. He was a vegan himself. He talked to me about Dr. Michael Greger, Neal Barnard, and the greats who had been working on preventive medicine and the Physicians Committee for Responsible Medicine.',
 'This was even before I understood the nuances of a medical college or had even seen a medical college for the first time.',
 'He showed me Cowspiracy, Seaspiracy and all these movies that showed us what happens when you consume a lot of meat and other things that, in several instances, didn’t have solid scientific data behind them.',
 'I was skeptical about a lot of this, but yet I listened to him because whole-food, plant-based diets and whole-food meals did have substance to them. And I kept that close to my heart.',
 ('h','The crossroads'),
 'I was at a crossroads in my life. I was very infuriated and washed away by the amount of time invested into NEET UG preparation during my 11th and 12th-grade schooling. I had enough of it.',
 'After my first attempt, I had enough marks to get into veterinary medicine or BDS, and I had decided that I would take whatever I got.',
 'But this individual and what he told me about whole-food diets, the power of preventive medicine and the influence of nutrition on life got me thinking.',
 'I told my mom and dad that I wanted to give it another go. And that’s how I decided to drop and repeat for an MBBS seat.',
 'I didn’t know then that these ideas would eventually become the foundation of my interest in research.',
 ('h','Then I joined college, and forgot'),
 'When I joined college, I was actually consumed by the need to fit in, to be validated, to be understood, to be loved, and to gain a place among my peers.',
 'I discussed these ideas with some of them in the initial days, but I was ridiculed for a while. I guess I just pushed them all out and focused on the things that were happening and very much the hype at the moment.',
 'Being isolated from college for a while due to COVID, then being back home, then back again, getting COVID twice, and trying to understand college dynamics, politics, arts and all the nuances of college life took up a lot of my attention.',
 'I kind of let all of that aside.',
 'I forgot about that guardian angel who actually took me to medical college. I forgot all the whys and hows of why I got there.',
 'I put a lot of time into politics, arguing, understanding college dynamics and things like that.',
 'But the overall atmosphere, when I look back, did not foster much academic thinking or even discourse.',
 'There were a lot of pretty faces, a lot of pretty decisions that are a regret, but you could say that all these decisions led me to where I am right now.',
 ])
R02=dict(id='R02',slug='nutrition-oncology-and-the-ncd-project',title='How Do You Do a Cross-Sectional Study? What Worked, and What We Got Wrong.',
 hook='We collected a mountain of data and never published it.',
 abstract=[],keywords='cross-sectional study; non-communicable diseases; WHO STEPS; institutional research committee; ethics committee; SPSS; undergraduate research',
 highlight='1',
 body=[
 ('h','Why we did it'),
 'Dr. Jerin James and I put a lot of time into working on this study. We did it because we felt it was a worthy enough research topic and it deserved our attention. And because the opportunity presented itself in the form of a health camp, where we would not otherwise get the data.',
 'We went through every step: the protocol, the study design, collecting a humongous amount of data, and then trying to analyse it. We could not crunch a paper out of it. It took a lot of our time in college, and we did not give an initial orientation, even to our guide, on how to go about structuring a research paper.',
 ('h','Narrow the topic first'),
 'For the cross-sectional study, the work was in narrowing down the research topic. Who we were studying, how we were going to study them, what guidelines or parent studies already existed, and finding an appropriate tool we could administer to get the data we needed.',
 ('g',[('Who','Define the population: which people, where, and how will you select them?'),('How','Choose the design and the setting.'),('Parent studies and guidelines','Find existing surveys and frameworks to build on, so you do not start from zero.'),('A tool','A validated questionnaire or instrument you can actually administer.')]),
 'I decided that I was going to study non-communicable diseases. So I started searching for templates that had the questionnaires and everything one might need to conduct the study.',
 ('h','Finding WHO STEPS'),
 'That is how I stumbled upon the WHO STEPS protocol, and I started making the questionnaire. I prepared the research protocol, and I was clueless as to how to collect the data, because WHO STEPS requires both biochemical data and anthropometric data.',
 ('g',[('WHO STEPS','The WHO STEPwise approach to NCD risk factor surveillance: a questionnaire, physical measurements and biochemical tests.'),('Anthropometry','Height, weight, waist circumference.'),('Biochemical data','Blood tests, which you cannot collect from a questionnaire alone.')]),
 ('h','Then, by coincidence, a health camp'),
 'Nearing third year, due to several setbacks, I was in a bout of depression, on medication and so forth. But yet, I saw an opportunity manifest before my eyes.',
 'Very coincidentally, the then College Union had decided to run a health camp. A lot of data would come in, a lot of tests would be done. Why waste it if we could assess the health metrics and risk factors of all the students on campus?',
 ('h','The Institutional Research Committee'),
 'We submitted our protocol to the IRC, the Institutional Research Committee. The committee reviews it, and the head of the IRC gave me corrections. I did the corrections, and after certain periodic reviews we were cleared for the IEC.',
 ('g',[('Submit the protocol','Full protocol to the Institutional Research Committee.'),('Take the corrections','The reviewer sends comments. Answer each one.'),('Periodic review','Resubmit until you are cleared.'),('Cleared for the IEC','Only then do you go to the ethics committee.')]),
 ('h','The Institutional Ethics Committee'),
 'We took a lot of printouts and submitted a request to the IEC. Then I went to the Institutional Ethics Committee meeting, where all the people on the committee reviewed the proposal, and our paper was ready for the Institutional Ethics Committee.',
 ('h','The study, and the mountain of data'),
 'So we did the study, and we were sitting on a mountain of data. We would go to each health camp, guide students through the camp, collect the data and measure their physical parameters, and we had the help of the whole college in doing so.',
 ('h','The analysis we were not ready for'),
 'We were unprepared as to how to analyse the data. We did not have ChatGPT or Claude at the time, so we worked manually, with SPSS, Excel and Google Sheets. We were very clueless, and depended heavily on Dr. Ashik, who taught us SPSS at every point.',
 'It took another one and a half years to get into the data, understand it and learn how to process it using SPSS and Excel formulas to break it down set by set. That took a lot of our time in the final year, particularly the eighth semester.',
 'We eventually managed to prepare a report and present it at national forums and undergraduate conferences in college. But we could never get around to publishing it because of the vast amount of data. We did have a guide who had published, but we did not orient the project with them well enough on how to go about structuring and handling it.',
 ('c','To this day, I believe we spread ourselves too thin.'),
 ('c','Too much data. Far too much data. Unsegmented, without the necessary tools or the team to understand the data, structure it properly or even publish it.'),
 'The project eventually earned me the KUHS UG Research Award, one of the first from my college. Dr. Jerin James and I spent the most time on it, and he is one of the reasons we were able to submit the report for the KUHS UG Research Award. But to my dismay, we were never able to publish it. That is something that will always bother me, and something I owe the people who made it possible, and the campus, for a while.',
 ])
